# Instructions for coding agents

This is a Databricks App: a React single-page app served by AppKit's Express
server, written in TypeScript. Every contributor starts with only this
repository, so everything that matters lives in it.

**"AppKit" here always means Databricks' TypeScript SDK,** the packages
`@databricks/appkit` (the server and its plugins) and `@databricks/appkit-ui`
(React components and data hooks). It is not Apple's AppKit framework, and
Apple's documentation answers the wrong questions. Look things up with
`npx @databricks/appkit docs <query>`, or at
https://databricks.github.io/appkit/llms.txt.

## How work is done here

- **The tools are the guardrail.** A rule that a tool can check is a check, and
  the build fails on it. Zero errors and zero warnings, always.
- **Every escape hatch needs a written reason.** A disable comment, a skipped
  test or a coverage exclusion is a debt that someone must be able to review.
- **Test first.** Work outside in: a scenario, then a failing test at the
  cheapest level that proves the rule, then the code.
- **Model the domain.** Business rules live in plain, immutable domain objects
  named in the business's own words.
- **Small commits,** each one passing the whole gate.

## Commands

| Command                  | What it does                                                                 |
| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `bin/setup`              | Installs the pinned toolchain, the dependencies and the git hooks            |
| `pnpm run check`         | **The Definition of Done.** Every check below except the smoke journeys      |
| `pnpm run lint`          | The fast checks: linters, architecture rules, formatting, SQL lint           |
| `pnpm run typecheck`     | `tsc -b`                                                                     |
| `pnpm test`              | Unit, component and page-level tests, with coverage and accessibility checks |
| `pnpm run test:e2e`      | Journeys through the built app, with Databricks faked                        |
| `pnpm run test:mutation` | Mutation testing of the code whose tests run in Node                         |
| `pnpm run test:smoke`    | Smoke journeys against the deployed app; needs workspace credentials         |
| `pnpm run typegen`       | Regenerates the types for the SQL files in `config/queries/`                 |

Keep the names `build`, `typecheck`, `lint`, `test`, `sync` and `typegen`: the
Databricks CLI calls them, and `databricks apps deploy` runs `build`,
`typecheck` and `lint` before every deploy, so `lint` must stay fast.

## The shape of the code

| Layer       | Folders                                       | Holds                                                                                   |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| View        | `client/src/pages/`, `client/src/components/` | Function components. No business logic, no `fetch`                                      |
| Application | `client/src/hooks/`, `client/src/acl/`        | Hooks. Only `acl/` touches AppKit's data hooks, its generated query types, and `/api/*` |
| Domain      | `client/src/domain/`                          | Plain TypeScript classes and functions. Nothing from React, AppKit or the browser       |
| Server      | `server/`                                     | AppKit's server, its plugins, and the app's own routes                                  |

The folders `acl/`, `domain/` and `server/` have their own `AGENTS.md`; read it
before editing there. Why the layers exist, and the exceptions AppKit's own
files need, are in `docs/design/architecture.md`.

**No request waterfalls.** Details and examples are in
`docs/design/data-loading.md`.

- Data from the app's own endpoints is loaded in route loaders, through
  TanStack Query, so every fetch starts in parallel before the page renders. A
  child route's loader never waits for its parent's data.
- AppKit's data hooks can't run in a loader. Call them, through their `acl/`
  hooks, at the top of each page component or in components that always render.
  Never hide a component that fetches behind another query's loading state;
  show the loading state beside it.
- Prefer one SQL query with a join to several queries that depend on one
  another. Each trip to a SQL warehouse costs seconds.

## Never

- `useEffect` with `fetch` to load data. Use a route loader or an `acl/` hook.
- `useMemo` or `useCallback`. The React Compiler memoizes.
- Changing a domain object. Write `order.withStatus(s)`, which returns a new
  object, not `order.status = s`.
- A custom endpoint for a warehouse `SELECT` or for Unity Catalog file
  operations. Put the query in `config/queries/` and read it with AppKit's
  hooks. Custom endpoints are for writes, Databricks APIs that have no plugin,
  logic over several steps, external services and file processing.
- Lakebase SQL in `config/queries/`. That folder is for the SQL warehouse only.
- Hand-written types for SQL results. Run `pnpm run typegen`.
- Next.js, React Router's framework mode, or any other server-rendering
  framework. AppKit's server is the server (`docs/decisions/`, record 0001).
- An `async` Express route handler that isn't wrapped to pass its errors to
  `next`. AppKit runs Express 4, which drops them.
- Forcing an install, or downgrading a package, to get past an install error.
  Stop and report it.
- A write path (Lakebase, `INSERT`s into Delta, or another system) before the
  team chooses where writes go (`docs/open-questions.md`).

## Escape hatches

Don't reach for a disable comment, `@ts-expect-error`, a skipped or focused
test, a coverage or mutation exclusion, or an entry in a tool's ignore list.
Fix the code instead. When one is truly unavoidable, it carries a description
that says why, on the same line, and the change says so in its commit message.

## Where knowledge lives

| Knowledge                                          | Home                                                                       |
| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Rules for every agent                              | This file                                                                  |
| Rules for one layer                                | `AGENTS.md` in that layer's folder                                         |
| The few decisions that shape the system            | `docs/decisions/`, written with the `adr` skill                            |
| How the system works, and why each tool was chosen | `docs/design/`                                                             |
| Platform facts, each with the date it was checked  | `docs/design/platform.md`                                                  |
| Questions only the team can answer                 | `docs/open-questions.md`                                                   |
| The work ahead                                     | `docs/plan.md`                                                             |
| Procedures worth repeating                         | `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, written with the `skill-authoring` skill |

Shared skills (Databricks' own, `adr`, `skill-authoring`) are pinned in
`skills-lock.json` and installed by a git hook; never commit their files.

## Definition of Done

- `pnpm run check` passes with zero errors and zero warnings.
- Each new test fails without the change, checks specific behavior, and
  survives mutation testing.
- Documents that describe changed behavior are updated in the same commit.
- `docs/plan.md` is ticked off if the work finished an item.
- A commit message is proposed in the Conventional Commits form, explaining why
  rather than listing files.

## Before you stop

Everything the next contributor needs is committed in its home from the table
above. Nothing that matters lives only in a conversation, a scratch file or one
tool's private memory.

## Committing

What agents may do with git without asking is the team's decision, and it is
still open (`docs/open-questions.md`). Until it is answered, commit and push
nothing without asking.
